Instance configurations for timberfs's template units live in this directory.

Each <instance>.conf defines the environment for one timberfs mount:

    BACKING=/var/log/timberfs-backing/applogs
    MOUNTPOINT=/var/log/apps
    EXTRA_OPTS=--allow-other

Then: systemctl enable --now timberfs@<instance>

Each otlp-<instance>.conf defines one OTLP shipper — a store, and the
OTLP/HTTP receiver its entries are posted to:

    STORE=/var/log/timberfs-backing/applogs/app.log
    ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4318
    EXTRA_OPTS=--service applogs

Then: systemctl enable --now timberfs-otlp@<instance>

text.conf and text-<instance>.conf configure the plain-text FIFO intake —
for a producer that can only log to a path (Apache, nginx). text.conf holds
the defaults for every instance and text-<instance>.conf overrides them for
one, key by key:

    STORE_DIR=/var/log/timberfs
    DECLARE=index=true retain=90d

Then: systemctl enable --now timberfs-text@<instance>.socket

DECLARE is applied to the store on every start, so retention lives here
rather than in someone's shell history. The store is
STORE_DIR/<instance>/<instance>.log: one directory per store, named after
the store, which is the name you query it by.

follow-<instance>.conf configures one file follower (timberfs-follow@) — for
a producer that keeps writing its own log file while timberfs reads it. SOURCE
is required; follow.conf holds the defaults for every instance:

    SOURCE=/var/log/exim4/mainlog
    DECLARE=index=true retain=90d format=exim-main

Then: systemctl enable --now timberfs-follow@exim-main

The remaining units are configured with a systemd drop-in instead — the
records-format log intake (systemctl edit timberfs-log@<instance>.service)
and the two network intakes, which are not templated because one listener
serves every stream (systemctl edit timberfs-forward.service,
timberfs-otlp.service).

forests.d/*.conf lists the directories searched when a command is given a
bare handle instead of a path (see FORESTS in man 1 timberfs).

limits.conf OVERRIDES what this machine will let ONE REQUEST ask for —
MAX_ENTRIES, MAX_CHUNKS, DEADLINE_MS. The ceilings are on by default and
this file changes them, key by key; `timberfs limits` prints what is in
force. It bounds a query DOCUMENT, not the flags beside it (see QUERY
CEILINGS in man 1 timberfs).

Commented examples: /usr/share/doc/timberfs/examples/
Manuals: man 1 timberfs, man 1 timber-otlp
